<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, study of extreme values of bounds in topological indices plays an important role in determining the impact of these indices on trees and bipartite graphs. For any integers <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,p</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq p\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq p\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3</annotation></semantics></math>, the upper bound of the minimum value of Albertson index <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy:</p>
    
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)\frac{2^{\alpha}}{(\Delta-p)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}\geqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)\frac{2^{\alpha}}{(\Delta-p)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">Studies the maximum and minimum values of the Albertson index in bipartite graphs based on the sizes of their bipartition sets. Leveraging a known corollary, explicit formulas and bounds are derived that characterize the irregularity measure under various vertex size conditions. Upper bounds for both the Albertson and Sigma indices are established using graph parameters such as degrees and edge counts. Theoretical results are supported by examples and inequalities involving degree sequences, enhancing understanding of extremal irregularity behavior in bipartite graphs.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper. Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a simple, connected graph, where <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|V(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|V(G)|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=|E(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=|E(G)|</annotation></semantics></math>, the minimum and the maximum degree of <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta,\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta,\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leqslant\delta&lt;\Delta\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⩽</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leqslant\delta&lt;\Delta\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V_{1},V_{2},E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V_{1},V_{2},E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph with two vertex sets <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}=|V_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}=|V_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}=|V_{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}=|V_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, if</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg_{G}(v_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\deg_{G}(v_{j})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg_{G}(v_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\deg_{G}(v_{j}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">we say <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is bipartite graph. Optimally, <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is bipartite graph if its vertices can be partitioned into two sets such that every edge connects a vertex in <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> to a vertex in <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The maximum degree of a node had been defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> where the symmetric nodes are equal and we denote that by <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\max[\deg(u)]=|V|\text{ or }\max[\deg(V)]=|u|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\max[\deg(u)]=|V|\text{ or }\max[\deg(V)]=|u|)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, we say:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum[\deg(u)]=|V|\text{ or }\sum[\deg(V)]=|u|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum[\deg(u)]=|V|\text{ or }\sum[\deg(V)]=|u|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a path of order <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the star tree of order <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, typically denoted by <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1,n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1,n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In 1997, Albertson in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> mention to the imbalance of an edge <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="imb(uv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">imb(uv)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the irregularity (Albertson index) measure defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> as:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}\lvert\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)\rvert." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}\lvert\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> the maximum degree of Albertson index and <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> the minimum degree of Albertson index. Ali, A. et al in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> mention to total irregularity of <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}(G)=(\deg(v_{1}),\deg(v_{2}),\dots,\deg(v_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}(G)=(\deg(v_{1}),\deg(v_{2}),\dots,\deg(v_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> a degree sequence of <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(v_{1})\geqslant\deg(v_{2})\geqslant\dots\geqslant\deg(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(v_{1})\geqslant\deg(v_{2})\geqslant\dots\geqslant\deg(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)=2(n+1)m-2\sum_{i=1}^{n}i\deg(v_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)=2(n+1)m-2\sum_{i=1}^{n}i\deg(v_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">when <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}(G)=(k,k,\ldots,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}(G)=(k,k,\ldots,k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is regular of degree <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, the graph is irregular. Ghalavand, A. et al. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> proved <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{T}(G)\leq n^{2}\operatorname{irr}(G)/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{T}(G)\leq n^{2}\operatorname{irr}(G)/4</annotation></semantics></math>, when the bound is sharp for infinitely many graphs. The first and the second Zagreb index, <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(G)=\sum_{v\in V(G)}\deg(v)^{2},\quad\text{and}\quad M_{2}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}\deg(u)\deg(v)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(G)=\sum_{v\in V(G)}\deg(v)^{2},\quad\text{and}\quad M_{2}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}\deg(u)\deg(v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Sigma index had defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}(\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v))^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(G)=\sum_{uv\in E(G)}(\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v))^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> the maximum degree of Sigma index and <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> the minimum degree of Sigma index. Bipartite graphs are additionally employed to analyse algebraic graphs, which have associative ring elements as vertices and edges. The study of algebraic graphs has applications in extreme graph theory and algebraic cryptography. Additionally, bipartite graphs are utilized in many fields of mathematics; for example, network flow and matching algorithms. The combinatorial foundation of these algorithms is based on the study of bipartite graphs, and the near-equivalence of network flow and matching algorithms is a key notion in this discipline (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal of this paper, study of the extreme bounds of the two indices — Albertson index and Sigma index — as well as the study of these indices on bipartite graph theory, and discussion of these cases when the bipartite graph is complete and when it is incomplete.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we review the important concepts relevant to our work, including a literature survey of the most related papers. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we provide a preface covering some of the key theories and properties we have utilized to understand the work. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we introduce some bounds involving the extremal values of the Albertson and Sigma Indices. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we employ results on the bounds of the Albertson index and Sigma index via bipartite graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we explore important concepts and recent advancements in graph irregularity indices, with a particular emphasis on trees. Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a class of graphs, then we have <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\max},\operatorname{irr}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\max},\operatorname{irr}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> of Albertson index in a graph <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, where:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(\mathcal{S})=\max\{\operatorname{irr}(G)\mid G\in\mathcal{S}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(\mathcal{S})=\max\{\operatorname{irr}(G)\mid G\in\mathcal{S}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(\mathcal{S})=\min\{\operatorname{irr}(G)\mid G\in\mathcal{S}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(\mathcal{S})=\min\{\operatorname{irr}(G)\mid G\in\mathcal{S}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Yang J, Deng H., M., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> provide <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq p\leq n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq p\leq n-3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then: <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(G)=n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(G)=n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. For Caterpillar tree with path vertices with degrees <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},d_{2},\dots,d_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},d_{2},\dots,d_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)=\left({{d_{n}}-1}\right)^{2}+\left({d_{1}-1}\right)^{2}+\sum\limits_{i=2}^{n-1}{\left({{d_{i}}-1}\right)\left({{d_{i}}-2}\right)}+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)=\left({{d_{n}}-1}\right)^{2}+\left({d_{1}-1}\right)^{2}+\sum\limits_{i=2}^{n-1}{\left({{d_{i}}-1}\right)\left({{d_{i}}-2}\right)}+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|d_{i}-d_{i+1}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> 
The star <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the only tree of order n that has a great deal of irregularity, satisfying:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}\left({{S_{n}}}\right)=\left({n-2}\right)\left({n-1}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}\left({{S_{n}}}\right)=\left({n-2}\right)\left({n-1}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the case where <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree, the <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">general Albertson irregularity index</span> of a graph was defined by Lina et al. in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> as, for any <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{p}(G)=\left(\sum_{uv\in E(G)}|\deg(u)-\deg(v)|^{p}\right)^{\frac{1}{p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{p}(G)=\left(\sum_{uv\in E(G)}|\deg(u)-\deg(v)|^{p}\right)^{\frac{1}{p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then, in next Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2 ([]). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, we provide the maximum degree of the total Albertson index and the minimum degree as we show that in theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, the total Albertson index among <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{t}(G)=\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{t_{\max}}(G)=(n-1)(n-2),\\
\operatorname{irr}_{t_{\min}}(G)=2(n-2).\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{t}(G)=\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{t_{\max}}(G)=(n-1)(n-2),\\
\operatorname{irr}_{t_{\min}}(G)=2(n-2).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By computing constraints on all degree sequences of <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex graphs, the degree sequence that maximizes this bound corresponds to a graph <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with a maximum irregularity. Using variance, Mandal et al. in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> showed:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sqrt{m\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}d_{i}^{2}\right)-4m^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sqrt{m\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}d_{i}^{2}\right)-4m^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of edges.
In Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 2.1 (Upper bound of Sigma index []). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, we observe an upper bound of Sigma index with maximum degree <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the minimum degree <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> by considering determine terms where <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta-\delta)^{3}&gt;(\Delta-1)^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta-\delta)^{3}&gt;(\Delta-1)^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Upper bound of Sigma index <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any graph connected <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices with minimal degree <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, maximal degree <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and maximal <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> irregularity. Then, the upper bound of <math id="S2.Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as:</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(G)&gt;\frac{\delta}{\Delta+1}(\Delta-\delta)^{3}n,\quad\sigma(G)&gt;\frac{1}{\Delta+1}(\Delta-1)^{3}n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(G)&gt;\frac{\delta}{\Delta+1}(\Delta-\delta)^{3}n,\quad\sigma(G)&gt;\frac{1}{\Delta+1}(\Delta-1)^{3}n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let be <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> a classes of trees, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{max},\sigma_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{max},\sigma_{min}</annotation></semantics></math> in trees with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices by:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathcal{T})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}{\sigma_{\max}(\mathcal{T})}=\left({n-1}\right)\left({n-2}\right){\rm{}};n\geq 3\\
{\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal{T})}=0{\rm{;n=2}.}\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>min</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathcal{T})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}{\sigma_{\max}(\mathcal{T})}=\left({n-1}\right)\left({n-2}\right){\rm{}};n\geq 3\\
{\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal{T})}=0{\rm{;n=2}.}\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a connected graph <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, if it is not a complete graph, then in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> defined the bound on <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="~\sigma_{2}(G)=\min\{\deg_{G}(u)+\deg_{G}(u)\mid uv\notin E(G),u,v\in V(G)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma_{2}(G)=\min\{\deg_{G}(u)+\deg_{G}(u)\mid uv\notin E(G),u,v\in V(G)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Actually, according to (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2 Preliminaries ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), the upper bound <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}(G)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}(G)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> satisfied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> when <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is hamiltonian with <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Complete bipartite graph had given in Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (Complete bipartite graph []). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where we support that with Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to show the difference between bipartite graph and complete bipartite graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Complete bipartite graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V_{1},V_{2},E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V_{1},V_{2},E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph. Then <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{s,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{s,t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete bipartite graph where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V_{1}(G)|=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V_{1}(G)|=s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V_{2}(G)|=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V_{2}(G)|=t</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we presented a bipartite graph and complete bipartite graph for shown the difference between them.</p>
</div>
