<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth mentioning that according to (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.1 Some Bounds of Albertson Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) for Albertson index when <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)&gt;\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)&gt;\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\sqrt{2n(\Delta^{2}+3m}/2(\Delta+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\sqrt{2n(\Delta^{2}+3m}/2(\Delta+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(T)\leqslant\lambda\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(T)\leqslant\lambda\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we show in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In 3.1 Some Bounds of Albertson Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) an upper bound for the maximum value of Albertson index by comparing with the minimum vale as:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\lambda\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\geqslant\operatorname{irr}_{\min}-\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)\frac{2^{\alpha}}{(\Delta-p)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\lambda\operatorname{irr}_{\max}\geqslant\operatorname{irr}_{\min}-\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)\frac{2^{\alpha}}{(\Delta-p)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
