<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> be an average of vertex degree. Then, the bound Albertson index satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(T)\geqslant\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)-\frac{2m}{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(T)\geqslant\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)-\frac{2m}{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of vertices of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with degrees <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{T}(v_{1}),\dots,\deg_{T}(v_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{T}(v_{1}),\dots,\deg_{T}(v_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the average vertex degree. We know that <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=m+1</annotation></semantics></math>, thus <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)&gt;\frac{2m}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)&gt;\frac{2m}{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the upper bound of the Albertson index satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)\geqslant\frac{3\Delta m^{2}+2\delta m}{n(\Delta-3)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)\geqslant\frac{3\Delta m^{2}+2\delta m}{n(\Delta-3)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a star tree <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)=2-\frac{2}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)=2-\frac{2}{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, when <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a path tree <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we also have <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)=2-\frac{2}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)=2-\frac{2}{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, for any tree, the average vertex degree satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(T)&gt;\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)\geqslant\frac{2m}{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(T)&gt;\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)\geqslant\frac{2m}{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, from (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Some Bounds of Albertson Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.1 Some Bounds of Albertson Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), it follows that the bound for the difference between the average vertex degree and the term <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2m}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2m}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leqslant\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)-\frac{2m}{n}\leqslant\Delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leqslant\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)-\frac{2m}{n}\leqslant\Delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
