<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Some Bounds of Sigma Index</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through this subsection, we review some bounds according to the extreme values of the Sigma index under certain conditions, where through Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> we examine these bounds according to Condition <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmproposition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmproposition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer where <math id="S3.Thmproposition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the upper bound of the minimum Sigma index is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma_{\min}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{3}}{2}\rceil}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma_{\min}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{3}}{2}\rceil}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{D}=(d_{1},\dots,d_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree sequence where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}\leqslant\dots\leqslant d_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⩽</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⩽</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}\leqslant\dots\leqslant d_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. According to Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we obtain from (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) the following bound for the minimum Albertson index:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{irr}_{\min}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\right\rceil}{(d_{n}-d_{1})!+(\Delta-1)!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{irr}_{\min}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\right\rceil}{(d_{n}-d_{1})!+(\Delta-1)!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\sigma}\leq\mathrm{irr}\sqrt{m\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>σ</mi></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\sigma}\leq\mathrm{irr}\sqrt{m\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) satisfies <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2\sigma}\leq 2\mathrm{irr}\sqrt{2m\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>irr</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2\sigma}\leq 2\mathrm{irr}\sqrt{2m\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, the bound for the Albertson index is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\mathrm{irr}_{\min}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{3}}{2}\right\rceil}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\mathrm{irr}_{\min}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{3}}{2}\right\rceil}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq p\leq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq p\leq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. The terms satisfy <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\right\rceil\leq\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{3}}{2}\right\rceil\leq\cdots\leq\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{p}}{2}\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\right\rceil\leq\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{3}}{2}\right\rceil\leq\cdots\leq\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{p}}{2}\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{p}\leq 1+\Delta+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{p}\leq 1+\Delta+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{2}}{2}\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. From (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), it follows that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{p}&lt;\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{p}}{2}\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{p}&lt;\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{p}}{2}\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;k&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;k&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=n-\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=n-\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{p}\leq 2^{k}\leq 1+\Delta+\Delta^{2}+\cdots+\Delta^{p}+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{p}}{2}\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{p}\leq 2^{k}\leq 1+\Delta+\Delta^{2}+\cdots+\Delta^{p}+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{p}}{2}\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\min}&gt;2^{\alpha}\frac{2^{k}}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\min}&gt;2^{\alpha}\frac{2^{k}}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\alpha}\frac{2^{k}}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{3}}{2}\right\rceil}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\alpha}\frac{2^{k}}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}\geqslant 2^{\alpha}\dfrac{2^{n-\Delta}+\left\lceil\frac{n\Delta^{3}}{2}\right\rceil}{\Delta(\Delta-2)!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, from (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), we derive an upper bound for the minimum Sigma index, consistent with (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> provide an upper bound of the minimum Sigma index. Also, another upper bound shown in (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Proof. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) that will employee to established another upper bound in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmproposition5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> as we show that.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmproposition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmproposition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmproposition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmproposition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="S3.Thmproposition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmproposition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer where <math id="S3.Thmproposition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq p\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq p\leq\alpha\leq\Delta-3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the upper bound of the minimum Sigma index is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma_{\min}\geqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)\frac{3^{\alpha}+\Delta^{p}}{(n-3\Delta)!+(\Delta-p)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma_{\min}\geqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)\frac{3^{\alpha}+\Delta^{p}}{(n-3\Delta)!+(\Delta-p)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Actually, both bounds (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) according to Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3.1 Some Bounds of Albertson Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> we provide another upper bound (<a href="#S3.E21" title="In 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) of minimum Sigma index involving with (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) by considering the term <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq p\leq\alpha\leq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq p\leq\alpha\leq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma_{\min}\geqslant n\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)\frac{3^{\alpha}}{(2\Delta-3p)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma_{\min}\geqslant n\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)\frac{3^{\alpha}}{(2\Delta-3p)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathcal{T}_{n,\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathcal{T}_{n,\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\ncong\mathcal{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\ncong\mathcal{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for Sigma index satisfy <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(T)\geq\sigma(\mathcal{P}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(T)\geq\sigma(\mathcal{P}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\cong\mathcal{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\cong\mathcal{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Actually, Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.2 Some Bounds of Sigma Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> had given immediately according to Lemma‘<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Some Bounds of Albertson Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> for Albertson index by considering the term <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\sigma}\leqslant\operatorname{irr}\leqslant\sqrt{m\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>σ</mi></msqrt><mo>⩽</mo><mi>irr</mi><mo>⩽</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\sigma}\leqslant\operatorname{irr}\leqslant\sqrt{m\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree of order <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> be an average of vertex degree. Then, the bound of Sigma index satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(T)\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(T)+\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)^{2}-\frac{2m^{2}}{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(T)\geqslant\operatorname{irr}(T)+\deg_{\mathrm{ave}}(v)^{2}-\frac{2m^{2}}{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">According to Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Some Bounds of Albertson Index ‣ 3 Bounds with Extremal Value of Albertson and Sigma Indices ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> for Albertson index. By using the properties of the Albertson index on the Sigma index, and to avoid repetition, we obtain the desired bound.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide among Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Corollary 4.1 ([]). ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> a bound of Albertson index with certain terms, which it established Theorem <a href="#S4.E23" title="In Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}\geqslant n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⩾</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}\geqslant n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the cardinalities of the bipartite sets, respectively, for the bipartite graph <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)\leqslant u_{1}u_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})+u_{1}(n_{2}-u_{2})(n_{2}-u_{1})+u_{2}(n_{1}-u_{1})(n_{1}-u_{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)\leqslant u_{1}u_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})+u_{1}(n_{2}-u_{2})(n_{2}-u_{1})+u_{2}(n_{1}-u_{1})(n_{1}-u_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex31X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex31X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{1}=\frac{1}{2}(n_{2}-u_{2})\quad\text{and}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{1}=\frac{1}{2}(n_{2}-u_{2})\quad\text{and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex31Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex31Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{2}=\begin{cases}n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}&amp;\text{if }n_{1}&lt;2n_{2},\\
n_{2}&amp;\text{if }n_{1}\geqslant 2n_{2}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{2}=\begin{cases}n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}&amp;\text{if }n_{1}&lt;2n_{2},\\
n_{2}&amp;\text{if }n_{1}\geqslant 2n_{2}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">According to corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Corollary 4.1 ([]). ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> we provide Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for the maximum and minimum value of Albertson index.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}\leq 2n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}\leq 2n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be given as:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\frac{4}{3}n_{1}-\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{2}n_{1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\frac{4}{3}n_{1}-\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{2}n_{1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the Albertson index is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\{n_{1},n_{2}\}}(G)=\frac{3}{4}\lambda^{3}+\frac{n_{2}}{2^{n_{2}}}\lambda^{n_{2}}+\frac{\lambda n_{2}^{2}}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\{n_{1},n_{2}\}}(G)=\frac{3}{4}\lambda^{3}+\frac{n_{2}}{2^{n_{2}}}\lambda^{n_{2}}+\frac{\lambda n_{2}^{2}}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, the maximum and minimum values of the Albertson index are:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)=\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(G)=\frac{1}{108}\left(4n_{1}-\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}\right)^{3}+\alpha n_{1}^{2}-\alpha^{2}n_{1},\\
\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(G)=\frac{4}{3}n_{1}-\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{2}n_{1}},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>108</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)=\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(G)=\frac{1}{108}\left(4n_{1}-\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}\right)^{3}+\alpha n_{1}^{2}-\alpha^{2}n_{1},\\
\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(G)=\frac{4}{3}n_{1}-\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{2}n_{1}},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph. The Albertson index of <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, given in (<a href="#S4.E24" title="In Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) and derived from Equation <a href="#S4.E23" title="In Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>, satisfies the following maximum and minimum values according to Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Corollary 4.1 ([]). ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(G)=\frac{1}{2}(n_{2}-u_{2}),\\
\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(G)=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(G)=\frac{1}{2}(n_{2}-u_{2}),\\
\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(G)=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">From (<a href="#S4.E26" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E24" title="In Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>), for the term <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n_{2}-u_{2})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n_{2}-u_{2})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, since <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we observe that <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}n_{1}^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}n_{1}^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}^{2}n_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}^{2}n_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the Albertson index satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\frac{1}{4}(n_{2}-u_{2})^{3}+u_{2}n_{1}^{2}-u_{2}^{2}n_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\frac{1}{4}(n_{2}-u_{2})^{3}+u_{2}n_{1}^{2}-u_{2}^{2}n_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">This expression is complex due to the cubic term and the square root in <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Albertson index in (<a href="#S4.E27" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) satisfies <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)\geqslant\min\{n_{1},n_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)\geqslant\min\{n_{1},n_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, given that <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{n_{1},n_{2}\}\geqslant\min\{n_{1},n_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{n_{1},n_{2}\}\geqslant\min\{n_{1},n_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\alpha n_{1}^{2}-\alpha^{2}n_{1}\geqslant\max\{n_{1},n_{2}\}&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⩾</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\alpha n_{1}^{2}-\alpha^{2}n_{1}\geqslant\max\{n_{1},n_{2}\}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, from (<a href="#S4.E23" title="In Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E27" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>), the maximum value of the Albertson index is:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(G)=\frac{1}{108}\left(4n_{1}-\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}\right)^{3}+\alpha n_{1}^{2}-\alpha^{2}n_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>108</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(G)=\frac{1}{108}\left(4n_{1}-\sqrt{28n_{1}^{2}-24n_{1}n_{2}}\right)^{3}+\alpha n_{1}^{2}-\alpha^{2}n_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">For the minimum value of the Albertson index, referring to (<a href="#S4.E26" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>), we have:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(G)=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(G)=n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}&lt;2n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}&lt;2n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows from (<a href="#S4.E23" title="In Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) that for some parameter <math id="S4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\lambda\leqslant\min\{n_{1},n_{2}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\lambda\leqslant\min\{n_{1},n_{2}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.7.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, from (<a href="#S4.E29" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>), Equation (<a href="#S4.E26" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) holds as:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{\max}=\max\left\{\frac{1}{2}(n_{2}-u_{2})\right\},\\
\operatorname{irr}_{\min}=\min\left\{n_{2},n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}\right\}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\begin{cases}\operatorname{irr}_{\max}=\max\left\{\frac{1}{2}(n_{2}-u_{2})\right\},\\
\operatorname{irr}_{\min}=\min\left\{n_{2},n_{2}-\frac{4}{3}n_{1}+\sqrt{\frac{28}{9}n_{1}^{2}-\frac{8}{3}n_{1}n_{2}}\right\}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.8.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, by determining the maximum value of the Albertson index in (<a href="#S4.E29" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) and based on the conditions given in (<a href="#S4.E26" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>)–(<a href="#S4.E31" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>), the minimum value of the Albertson index is achieved as shown in (<a href="#S4.E25" title="In Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>), completing the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The study of extremal values in bipartite graphs is important because it helps characterize the maximum or minimum values of key graph parameters—such as number of edges, degrees, entropy, or absence of certain subgraphs—under specific constraints. This area is a core part of extremal graph theory, which explores how global graph properties constrain or influence local structures and parameters. Using Properties <a href="#S4.Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we provide a definition of these extreme values in the bipartite graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a class of graphs. Then, for <math id="S4.Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}_{\max},\operatorname{irr}_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}_{\max},\operatorname{irr}_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> Albertson index of a graph <math id="S4.Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, where:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(\mathcal{S})=\max\{\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\mid\mathscr{G}\in\mathcal{S}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}_{\max}(\mathcal{S})=\max\{\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\mid\mathscr{G}\in\mathcal{S}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(\mathcal{S})=\min\{\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\mid\mathscr{G}\in\mathcal{S}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>irr</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{irr}_{\min}(\mathcal{S})=\min\{\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\mid\mathscr{G}\in\mathcal{S}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we introduce the maximum and minimum Sigma index in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be a class of graphs. Then, we have <math id="S4.Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max},\sigma_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max},\sigma_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> of a graph <math id="S4.Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, where:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{\max}(\mathcal{S})=\max\{\sigma(\mathscr{G})\mid\mathscr{G}\in\mathcal{S}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{\max}(\mathcal{S})=\max\{\sigma(\mathscr{G})\mid\mathscr{G}\in\mathcal{S}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal{S})=\min\{\sigma(\mathscr{G})\mid\mathscr{G}\in\mathcal{S}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal{S})=\min\{\sigma(\mathscr{G})\mid\mathscr{G}\in\mathcal{S}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Among Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we provide an upper bound of both indices by considering the cases of bipartite graph and complete bipartite graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph with partition <math id="S4.Thmproposition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmproposition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}=|V_{1}|,n_{2}=|V_{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}=|V_{1}|,n_{2}=|V_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math> with maximum degree <math id="S4.Thmproposition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and minimum degree <math id="S4.Thmproposition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmproposition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> edges. Then, Albertson index is:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}+\frac{n_{2}\delta}{m},\quad\sigma(\mathscr{D})\leq 4n_{1}n_{2}+\frac{\sqrt{2n_{1}}n_{2}\delta}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}+\frac{n_{2}\delta}{m},\quad\sigma(\mathscr{D})\leq 4n_{1}n_{2}+\frac{\sqrt{2n_{1}}n_{2}\delta}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph, where <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq m(\Delta-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq m(\Delta-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq 2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq 2m</annotation></semantics></math> is always true <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)|\leq\deg_{G}(u)+\deg_{G}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)|\leq\deg_{G}(u)+\deg_{G}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E}|\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)|\leq\sum_{uv\in E}(\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v))=2m." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)=\sum_{uv\in E}|\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)|\leq\sum_{uv\in E}(\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v))=2m.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Alternatively, from (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) for Albertson index in <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)=\sum_{u\in V_{1}}\sum_{v\in V_{2}}a_{uv}|\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)=\sum_{u\in V_{1}}\sum_{v\in V_{2}}a_{uv}|\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{uv}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{uv}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
Therefore, by considering <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)|\leq\Delta-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\deg_{G}(u)-\deg_{G}(v)|\leq\Delta-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, when <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq n_{1}n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq n_{1}n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq n_{1}n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq n_{1}n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in a bipartite graph <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, from (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) we have:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}+\Delta(n_{1}-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}+\Delta(n_{1}-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">From (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E34" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>), we established the upper bound as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant 2m-\frac{n_{2}\delta}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant 2m-\frac{n_{2}\delta}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">The inequality in bound (<a href="#S4.E35" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) had given us in special case by considering All complete bipartite graphs that are trees are star graphs. In fact, every star graph <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the complete bipartite graph <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1,k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, when every star graph is a bipartite graph, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq\left(\left\lfloor\frac{2n_{1}}{m-3\Delta}\right\rfloor^{2}-1\right)+\left(\left\lfloor\frac{2n_{2}}{m-3\Delta}\right\rfloor^{2}-1\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq\left(\left\lfloor\frac{2n_{1}}{m-3\Delta}\right\rfloor^{2}-1\right)+\left(\left\lfloor\frac{2n_{2}}{m-3\Delta}\right\rfloor^{2}-1\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.6.1" class="ltx_text">Actually, when <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\leq n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\leq n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so, from (<a href="#S4.E35" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) the upper bound of Albertson index in <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq m(n_{2}-1)\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leq m(n_{2}-1)\leq 2n_{1}n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the first part of the inequality of Albertson index in (<a href="#S4.E32" title="In Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then, for Sigma index we established the inequality according to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\sigma(T)}\leqslant\operatorname{irr}(T)\leqslant\sqrt{2m\sigma(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\sigma(T)}\leqslant\operatorname{irr}(T)\leqslant\sqrt{2m\sigma(T)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\sigma(T)}\leqslant\operatorname{irr}(T)\leqslant\sqrt{2n_{1}\sigma(T)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\sigma(T)}\leqslant\operatorname{irr}(T)\leqslant\sqrt{2n_{1}\sigma(T)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n_{1}\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n_{1}\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>. According to (<a href="#S4.E34" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>), we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(\mathscr{D})\leq 4n_{1}n_{2}+\Delta(n_{1}-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(\mathscr{D})\leq 4n_{1}n_{2}+\Delta(n_{1}-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, from (<a href="#S4.E37" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>) and according to (<a href="#S4.E36" title="In Proof. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>) for Sigma index satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S4.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leq\left(\left\lfloor\frac{4n_{1}}{m-3\Delta}\right\rfloor^{2}-1\right)+\left(\left\lfloor\frac{4n_{2}}{m-3\Delta}\right\rfloor^{2}-1\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leq\left(\left\lfloor\frac{4n_{1}}{m-3\Delta}\right\rfloor^{2}-1\right)+\left(\left\lfloor\frac{4n_{2}}{m-3\Delta}\right\rfloor^{2}-1\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the second part of the inequality of Sigma index in (<a href="#S4.E32" title="In Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) holds.
This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmproposition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmproposition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmproposition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph with maximum degree <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(G)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(G)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the bipartition sets of <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}=|V_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}=|V_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}=|V_{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}=|V_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of edges in <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the Albertson index of <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S4.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\left(\deg_{G}(v_{i})-1\right)^{2}+\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\left(\deg_{G}(u_{j})-2\right)+2n_{1}\Delta," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\left(\deg_{G}(v_{i})-1\right)^{2}+\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\left(\deg_{G}(u_{j})-2\right)+2n_{1}\Delta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmproposition4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}\in V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}\in V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let the maximum degree <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(G)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(G)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>
implies that at least one vertex in <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has a degree of at least 3. The irregularity arises from differences in degrees across the bipartition. For each edge <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}u_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}u_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\deg(v_{i})-\deg(u_{j})|\leq|\deg(v_{i})-1|+|\deg(u_{j})-2|+1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\deg(v_{i})-\deg(u_{j})|\leq|\deg(v_{i})-1|+|\deg(u_{j})-2|+1,</annotation></semantics></math>
since adding and subtracting baseline degrees yields</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\deg(v_{i})-\deg(u_{j})|=|(\deg(v_{i})-1)-(\deg(u_{j})-2)+1|\leq|\deg(v_{i})-1|+|\deg(u_{j})-2|+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\deg(v_{i})-\deg(u_{j})|=|(\deg(v_{i})-1)-(\deg(u_{j})-2)+1|\leq|\deg(v_{i})-1|+|\deg(u_{j})-2|+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\sum_{u_{j}\sim v_{i}}\big(|\deg(v_{i})-1|+|\deg(u_{j})-2|+1\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\sum_{u_{j}\sim v_{i}}\big(|\deg(v_{i})-1|+|\deg(u_{j})-2|+1\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, to clarify these relationships, we can discuss the following cases:</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 1:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a simple bipartite graph <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{s,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{s,t}</annotation></semantics></math> complete bipartite graph, when <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=t</annotation></semantics></math> the lower bound of Albertson index is</p>
<table id="S4.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(K_{s,t})\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\left(\deg_{G}(v_{i})-1\right)^{2}+\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\left(\deg_{G}(u_{j})-2\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(K_{s,t})\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\left(\deg_{G}(v_{i})-1\right)^{2}+\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\left(\deg_{G}(u_{j})-2\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">From another perspective, if <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq t</annotation></semantics></math>, then by considering the constant term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n_{1}\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n_{1}\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n_{2}\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n_{2}\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in this case we obtain:</p>
<table id="S4.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(K_{s,t})\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\left(\deg_{G}(v_{i})-1\right)^{2}+2n_{1}\Delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(K_{s,t})\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\left(\deg_{G}(v_{i})-1\right)^{2}+2n_{1}\Delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Therefore, from (<a href="#S4.E40" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E41" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>) we find that (<a href="#S4.E39" title="In Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>) is valid.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 2:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> don’t be a complete bipartite graph. Note that each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> neighbors, and each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(u_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(u_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> neighbors. Therefore,</p>
<table id="S4.E42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg(v_{i})|\deg(v_{i})-1|+\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\deg(u_{j})|\deg(u_{j})-2|+\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg(v_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg(v_{i})|\deg(v_{i})-1|+\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\deg(u_{j})|\deg(u_{j})-2|+\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg(v_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(v_{i})\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(v_{i})\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(v_{i})|\deg(v_{i})-1|\leq(\deg(v_{i})-1)^{2}+\deg(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(v_{i})|\deg(v_{i})-1|\leq(\deg(v_{i})-1)^{2}+\deg(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S4.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\left(\deg_{G}(v_{i})-1\right)^{2}+\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\left(\deg_{G}(u_{j})-2\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(G)\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\left(\deg_{G}(v_{i})-1\right)^{2}+\sum_{j=1}^{n_{2}}\left(\deg_{G}(u_{j})-2\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(v_{i})\leq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(v_{i})\leq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg(v_{i})\leq n_{1}\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n_{1}}\deg(v_{i})\leq n_{1}\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is not complete bipartite, the inequality is strict and meaningful.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Actually, from (<a href="#S4.E42" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E43" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a>) holds  (<a href="#S4.E39" title="In Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>) is valid.
In both cases 1 and 2 by considering the constant term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n_{1}\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n_{1}\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n_{2}\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n_{2}\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> noticed that (<a href="#S4.E39" title="In Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>) is valid. This completes the proof.
∎
Both of Propositions <a href="#S4.Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> had provide us a bounds based on the vertices present in each part of the bipartite graph. Such bounds facilitate a deeper understanding of the structure of bipartite graphs and aid in optimizing solutions to real-world problems. Furthermore, in Example <a href="#Thmexample1" title="Example 1. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> we observe an example which explains the Propositions <a href="#S4.Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmproposition4" title="Proposition 4.4. ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1</span></span><span id="Thmexample1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph where <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}=\{u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}=\{u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}=\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}=\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> organized as:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}u_{1}--v_{2},u_{1}--v_{3},\dots,u_{1}--v_{m-1}\\
u_{2}--v_{3},u_{2}--v_{4},\dots,u_{2}--v_{m-1}\\
u_{3}--v_{3},u_{3}--v_{4},\dots,u_{3}--v_{m-1}\\
u_{4}--v_{4},u_{4}--v_{5},\dots,u_{4}--v_{m-2}\\
u_{5}--v_{3},u_{5}--v_{4},\dots,u_{5}--v_{m-3}\\
u_{6}--v_{4},u_{6}--v_{5},\dots,u_{6}--v_{m-2}\\
u_{7}--v_{5},u_{7}--v_{6},\dots,u_{7}--v_{m-4}\\
u_{8}--v_{6},u_{8}--v_{7},\dots,u_{8}--v_{m-4}\\
u_{9}--v_{7},u_{9}--v_{(}10),\dots,u_{9}--v_{m-4}\\
