<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 1:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is complete bipartite graph denote by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n_{1},n_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n_{1},n_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, noticed that each vertex in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}(K_{n_{1},n_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}(K_{n_{1},n_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{K_{n_{1},n_{2}}}(u)=n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{K_{n_{1},n_{2}}}(u)=n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where it is connected to all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> vertices in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}(K_{n_{1},n_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}(K_{n_{1},n_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Same case according to each vertex in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}(K_{n_{1},n_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}(K_{n_{1},n_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{K_{n_{1},n_{2}}}(v)=n_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{K_{n_{1},n_{2}}}(v)=n_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(u)-\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(v)=n_{1}-n-_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(u)-\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(v)=n_{1}-n-_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have</p>
<table id="S4.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}|n_{2}-n_{1}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}|n_{2}-n_{1}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since there are <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> edges, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(G)=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{2}-n_{1})^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(G)=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{2}-n_{1})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math> For dive into proving the inequality (<a href="#S4.E44" title="In Lemma 4.3. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>) noticed that according to (<a href="#S4.E45" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>) holds</p>
<table id="S4.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leq(n_{1}-1)(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}+(n_{2}-2)(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leq(n_{1}-1)(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}+(n_{2}-2)(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, by considering the value of the first Zagreb index <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(G)=n_{1}n_{2}^{2}+n_{2}n_{1}^{2}=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}+n_{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(G)=n_{1}n_{2}^{2}+n_{2}n_{1}^{2}=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}+n_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}n_{2}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}n_{2}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n_{2}-n_{1})^{2}\leq 2(n_{1}+n_{2})+(n_{1}+n_{2}-3)|n_{2}-n_{1}|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n_{2}-n_{1})^{2}\leq 2(n_{1}+n_{2})+(n_{1}+n_{2}-3)|n_{2}-n_{1}|.</annotation></semantics></math> We find that (<a href="#S4.E44" title="In Lemma 4.3. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>) according to (<a href="#S4.E45" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E46" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>) is valid.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 2:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a complete bipartite graph. It is a subgraph of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n_{1},n_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n_{1},n_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> with fewer edges, so some vertices in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> have degree <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{u}\leq n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{u}\leq n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}\leq n_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}\leq n_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The edge count satisfies <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=|E(G)|\leq n_{1}n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=|E(G)|\leq n_{1}n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each edge <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E</annotation></semantics></math>, noticed that,</p>
<table id="S4.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(G)=\sum_{uv\in E}(\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(u)^{2}-2\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(u)\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(v)+\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(v)^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(G)=\sum_{uv\in E}(\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(u)^{2}-2\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(u)\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(v)+\deg_{\mathscr{G}}(v)^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then, from (<a href="#S4.E47" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>) according to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math> we establish the lower bound of Sigma index. Naturally, when considering the general state of the Sigma index, we establish a lower bound given by the following relationship (<a href="#S4.E48" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>), taking into account every partition of the bipartite graph, which we assumed does not represent a complete bipartite graph. Then,</p>
<table id="S4.E48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leq M_{1}(\mathscr{G})+(n_{1}-1)(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leq M_{1}(\mathscr{G})+(n_{1}-1)(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Actually, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math> weights degree differences linearly, while <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math> weights them quadratically. The term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2M_{1}(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2M_{1}(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math> contributes heavily based on degree squares. To maximize <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math> under constraints. Therefore, from (<a href="#S4.E47" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E48" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) we have</p>
<table id="S4.E49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leq(n_{1}-1)\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+(n_{2}-2)\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leq(n_{1}-1)\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})+(n_{2}-2)\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Finally, since the inequality (<a href="#S4.E49" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>) is valid, so by adding the value of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(\mathscr{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(\mathscr{G})</annotation></semantics></math> is still valid. This completes the proof.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The inequality had given in Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 4.3. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> holds for complete bipartite graphs, often strictly. Equality may (<a href="#S4.E44" title="In Lemma 4.3. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>) occur in limiting cases, by considering <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1},n_{2}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1},n_{2}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, it is typically strict.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph with the maximum degree <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the minimum degree <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant 2n_{1}^{2}+\frac{\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+5\delta}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant 2n_{1}^{2}+\frac{\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+5\delta},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S4.E51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant 2n_{1}^{3}+\frac{\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant 2n_{1}^{3}+\frac{\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite graph with vertex sets <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any vertex <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(v)=n_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(v)=n_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for any vertex <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(u)=n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(u)=n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, we will discuss the following cases:</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 1:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete bipartite graph. In this case, the Albertson index in (<a href="#S4.E50" title="In Lemma 4.4. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>) satisfies</p>
<table id="S4.E52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}^{2}n_{2}-n_{2}^{2}n_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}^{2}n_{2}-n_{2}^{2}n_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Next, we establish the bound for the term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}^{2}n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}^{2}n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}^{2}n_{2}\leqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}^{2}n_{2}\leqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)</annotation></semantics></math>
according to (<a href="#S4.E52" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>) by considering the maximum degree <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and the minimum degree <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S4.E53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)+\frac{2n_{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}}+n_{2}^{2}n_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)+\frac{2n_{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}}+n_{2}^{2}n_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Similarly, for the term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}^{2}n_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}^{2}n_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}^{2}n_{1}\leqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}^{2}n_{1}\leqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1).</annotation></semantics></math>
Thus, we observe that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n_{1}&lt;\Delta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n_{1}&lt;\Delta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. According to (<a href="#S4.E53" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>), when <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}n_{2}&lt;2n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}n_{2}&lt;2n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geqslant 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geqslant 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the bound (<a href="#S4.E50" title="In Lemma 4.4. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>) is valid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now, for the Sigma index, we find that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> measures how imbalanced the two vertex sets are. Then, for the term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}^{3}n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}^{3}n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}^{3}n_{2}\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}^{3}n_{2}\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)</annotation></semantics></math>
with the additional constraint on the minimum degree of vertices as</p>
<table id="S4.E54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)-2(n_{1}n_{2})^{2}+\frac{4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}+n_{1}n_{2}^{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)-2(n_{1}n_{2})^{2}+\frac{4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}+n_{1}n_{2}^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Similarly, for the term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}^{3}n_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}^{3}n_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}^{3}n_{1}\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}^{3}n_{1}\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1),</annotation></semantics></math>
and according to (<a href="#S4.E54" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>), by considering the term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}n_{2}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}n_{2}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}n_{2}^{3}\leqslant 2n_{1}^{3}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}n_{2}^{3}\leqslant 2n_{1}^{3},</annotation></semantics></math>
we get</p>
<table id="S4.E55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)-2(n_{1}n_{2})^{2}+\frac{4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}+2n_{1}^{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)-2(n_{1}n_{2})^{2}+\frac{4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}+2n_{1}^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Therefore, from (<a href="#S4.E54" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E55" title="In Case 1: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>), we conclude that (<a href="#S4.E51" title="In Lemma 4.4. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>) is valid.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Case 2:</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a complete bipartite graph. In this case, the Albertson index satisfies the bound</p>
<table id="S4.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}|n_{1}-n_{2}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}|n_{1}-n_{2}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then, we have</p>
<table id="S4.E56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)+\delta(\delta-1)^{2}+\frac{\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+5\delta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>irr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\operatorname{irr}(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)+\delta(\delta-1)^{2}+\frac{\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+5\delta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By considering <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)&gt;\delta(\delta-1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)&gt;\delta(\delta-1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)&gt;2n_{1}^{2}&gt;\delta(\delta-1)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{2}(\Delta-1)&gt;2n_{1}^{2}&gt;\delta(\delta-1)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Thus, according to (<a href="#S4.E56" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">56</span></a>), the bound (<a href="#S4.E50" title="In Lemma 4.4. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>) is valid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the Sigma index, we find that when <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete bipartite graph, it satisfies</p>
<table id="S4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\mathscr{G})=n_{1}n_{2}(n_{1}-n_{2})^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">but when <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is not complete, we establish the bound</p>
<table id="S4.E57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+\delta(\delta-1)^{3}+\frac{\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">~\sigma(\mathscr{G})\leqslant\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+\delta(\delta-1)^{3}+\frac{\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)+4n_{2}^{2}}{n_{1}n_{2}+3\delta^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Similarly, as we showed for the Albertson index, for the term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n_{1}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n_{1}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)&gt;2n_{1}^{3}&gt;\delta(\delta-1)^{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{3}(\Delta-1)&gt;2n_{1}^{3}&gt;\delta(\delta-1)^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Thus, from (<a href="#S4.E57" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">57</span></a>) and considering (<a href="#S4.E56" title="In Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">56</span></a>) for the maximum degree and the minimum degree <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\geqslant 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\geqslant 3</annotation></semantics></math>, we find that (<a href="#S4.E51" title="In Lemma 4.4. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>) is valid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in both Cases 1 and 2, we notice that (<a href="#S4.E50" title="In Lemma 4.4. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E51" title="In Lemma 4.4. ‣ Case 2: ‣ 4.1 Some Bounds on Bipartite Graph ‣ 4 Maximum of Albertson Index in Bipartite Graph ‣ Extremal Degree Irregularity Bounds for Albertson and Sigma Indices in Trees and Bipartite Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>) are valid for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof.
∎</p>
</div>
