<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through this paper, we presented a study of bounds on the topological indices which we elected to the Sigma Index and the Albertson Index. For any tree <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the Albertson index and the Sigma index play significant roles in understanding structural properties of trees and bipartite graphs. The <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">effect of the extreme values</span> of the Albertson and Sigma indices reveals important information about the bounds on irregularity and structural variance in both trees and bipartite graphs, illustrating how these indices govern graph properties under extremal conditions. The <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">maximum and minimum values</span> of the bounds for each index have been characterized, providing precise limitations on these indices, which correspond to specific graph structures such as regular graphs and <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-semiregular bipartite graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These extreme values are instrumental in <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">studying bounds on bipartite graphs</span>, enabling the identification of extremal graphs which achieve these bounds and guiding further combinatorial investigations and applications in network analysis. This framework not only advances the theoretical understanding of these indices but also supports diverse applications involving the measurement of irregularity and connectivity in bipartite graphs and trees.</p>
</div>
</section>
